00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:58.320
War One, right? That's espionage. In 2009, Barack Obama became president. And Barack

00:20:58.320 --> 00:21:05.040
Obama in his eight years as president charged eight more Americans under the espionage act

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:11.920
for speaking to the media nearly three times the number of all U.S. presidents combined.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:20.960
Uh, that was not an accident. When Obama first became president, friends of mine who were working

00:21:20.960 --> 00:21:29.040
at the White House and at the CIA described to me knock down drag out fights taking place

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:35.760
in the National Security Council. Obama was furious about leaks. The way it was described to

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:43.520
me was that Obama had a Nixonian obsession with leaks and he was being egged on by John Brennan,

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:49.600
who at the time was the deputy national security advisor and then became director of the CIA.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:57.520
And so this is, this was music to Brennan's ears. This allowed Brennan to go after

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:06.560
anybody he wanted. And so in those eight short years, eight of us were arrested and charged

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:10.080
under the espionage act. Let me make one other point too that I think is very important.

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:15.440
Espionage is one of the greatest crimes with which an American can be charged.

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.960
And in many cases it carries with it the death penalty. So can you imagine giving

00:22:20.960 --> 00:22:26.640
an interview to ABC News, which is what I did, and being charged with a death penalty crime?

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:37.120
That's America in the 21st century. And you know, it's not just, it's not just Barack Obama.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:43.840
When Donald Trump was elected, you know, of course I was as anti-Trump as anybody else was,

00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:47.760
but when Donald Trump was elected, I gave an interview to Mint Press News.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:53.360
And they had interviewed me, they interviewed Tom Drake, the NSA whistleblower, they

00:22:53.360 --> 00:22:57.840
interviewed Jesslyn Radek, the Justice Department whistleblower, and they asked all of us the same

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:03.280
question, are things going to get better or worse under Donald Trump? And I said, I was the only one

00:23:03.280 --> 00:23:09.920
who said this and I was foolish and wrong in retrospect. But I said, I can't imagine

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:17.440
a scenario where anything could be worse than Barack Obama. I can't imagine that Donald Trump

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:24.240
would be worse. And then Donald Trump was worse. The only thing that saved us from Trump was the

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:32.320
fact that he was only in office for four years. I'll add one other thing too. Trump had this weird

00:23:32.320 --> 00:23:41.280
fascination with Julian and by extension with Ed Snowden. And so for my own part, I hired a

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:50.480
Republican lobbyist to try to get me a pardon. For whatever reason, the White House staff put

00:23:50.480 --> 00:23:58.000
Julian, Ed Snowden and I together. And in the final days of the Trump administration,

00:23:58.000 --> 00:24:04.320
you remember it was a period of chaos. The January 6th insurrection had just taken place.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:10.720
Trump was on trial in the Senate after being impeached for the second time by the House of

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:20.560
Representatives. And in those final days of the Trump administration, the staff sent pardon

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:28.880
requests from Julian, Ed and me together as a group. That if we were to be pardoned,

00:24:28.960 --> 00:24:35.280
he was going to pardon the three of us together. Someone, we don't know who, we'll never know who,

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:43.360
told Mitch McConnell. And Mitch McConnell flipped out like the way that could do that he is

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:50.560
and called the president and said, if you pardon Julian Assange and Ed Snowden,

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:56.560
I'm sure he had never heard of me. You will lose the vote in the Senate and you will be

00:24:56.640 --> 00:25:03.600
convicted. You'll lose every Republican Senator. And so at the very last minute,

00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:11.040
Trump killed the pardon that would have freed all three of us. And that was the end of it.

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:16.720
He's not going to get that kind of treatment. Julian's not going to get that kind of treatment

00:25:16.720 --> 00:25:23.360
from Joe Biden. Yeah, speaking of Donald Trump, right after the Vault 7 leaks,

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:29.920
it turns out that the CIA director Mike Pompeo and others in the administration

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:36.560
were actually pushing Donald Trump to assassinate Julian Assange for publishing those leaks.

00:25:36.560 --> 00:25:42.800
That's right. And there's background to that. The first time that a covert action program

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:50.560
was proposed to either kidnap or murder Julian Assange was by John Brennan in the last two

00:25:50.640 --> 00:26:00.240
years of the Obama administration. And then with Pompeo as CIA director, just after Brennan left

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:07.920
the position, it took on sort of a new life. Now, what you're talking about, Chris,

00:26:07.920 --> 00:26:15.280
is an article that appeared in Yahoo News in which they interviewed, count them 36

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:24.080
current and former intelligence officers. This isn't just some renegade CIA person calling it

00:26:24.080 --> 00:26:33.920
in a tip. This was real investigative journalism. And what the plan was was to flush Julian out

00:26:33.920 --> 00:26:40.000
of the Ecuadorian embassy to attempt to kidnap him and render him to the United States for

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:47.200
trial or if he resisted to shoot and kill him. An agreement apparently had been made between the

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:54.560
CIA and the British MI6 that the Brits would be the ones to do the actual killing. But the CIA said,

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:59.680
and this is very common in CIA operational planning, you have to have scenario A, B,

00:26:59.680 --> 00:27:07.440
C and D, that were he to be rescued by the Russians and bundled onto a Russian government

00:27:07.440 --> 00:27:14.080
plane, in which case he would be treated as like a diplomatic pouch would be treated.

00:27:14.080 --> 00:27:20.240
You can't mess with it. The CIA was going to shoot the tires out of the Russian plane

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:25.360
and create an international incident to keep it from taking off so they could snatch Julian.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:34.160
Well, I would have to ask then, how in the world this guy is going to get a fair trial?

00:27:34.880 --> 00:27:42.000
We're the ones who are trying to murder him. I just don't understand how this can even be allowed

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:50.160
to stand. And I hope that that his attorneys and I know his attorneys, both in the UK and in the

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:56.320
US, and these are some of the finest legal minds that money can buy. I hope that they're

00:27:56.320 --> 00:28:02.800
able to use this to their, to their advantage and win his freedom. It's, it's so outrageous

00:28:02.960 --> 00:28:06.640
to me. I just can't even contemplate it.

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:11.920
For sure, John. I kind of feel like the lengths at which, I mean, just there's so much of this,

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:17.200
but the lengths that they would go, the lengths that they would go to be able to capture Julian

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:22.960
Assange and potentially assassinate him speaks volumes. I mean, he's a journalist. That's all

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:27.680
he's doing is just putting out there, as you've said, the information that we all have the

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:33.440
right to know. So it says a lot to me about internally what the priorities of this government

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:37.840
really are. I mean, the price of this government to keep all of its dirty laundry safe and to keep

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:42.320
the status quo as it is to keep the system operating and functioning in the interest of

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:47.360
really a small tiny portion of the population of the wealthy. It really isn't for us in any way,

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:52.080
shape or form. And I think sending that message to the world to journalists around the globe

00:28:52.160 --> 00:28:57.280
and to activists too, around the country and around the globe, that if you in any way,

00:28:57.280 --> 00:29:02.880
shape or form, expose the truth of what the U.S. does, this will be what happens to you. I think

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:07.200
the unrelenting nature of it to me kind of speaks that. It's just really that is the

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:13.040
message being sent. And I am curious, John, you were exposed. I mean, you exposed the

00:29:13.040 --> 00:29:17.920
U.S. torture program and you were the only one who went to jail for it. And so for you,

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:22.880
also seeing this with Assange as well, I mean, what's your own kind of gut reaction on your own

00:29:22.880 --> 00:29:27.680
case? And if you could speak a bit more on your experiences through the process that you went

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:36.640
through and everything after exposing the torture program. You know what? I can sum up my entire

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:45.360
experience in the federal legal system with one thing that my lead attorney said to me.

00:29:46.320 --> 00:29:54.720
I really believed that I was innocent. And I convinced myself that once I could get in front

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:59.600
of a jury, they would realize how ridiculous this was that I had

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:07.600
done a public service. I hadn't committed a crime, right? Torture's illegal. I exposed an illegal

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:16.000
program. I was convinced that I would be found not guilty. So they made their best and final offer.

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:21.920
Two and a half years, I would do 23 months and then have the thing behind me. And I

00:30:22.720 --> 00:30:28.560
thought about it, not just thought about it. My wife and I stayed up literally all night long,

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:37.120
researching this law called the Intelligence Identities Protection Act. I was the first American

00:30:37.120 --> 00:30:43.920
charged with this, with this crime. And so there were no precedents. There were two articles from

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:50.560
the Harvard Law School Journal saying that the law was probably unconstitutional, but no one ever

00:30:50.560 --> 00:30:56.320
had standing to challenge it. So I decided I was going to turn this deal down and I was going

00:30:56.720 --> 00:31:02.400
go to trial. Now I was facing 45 years in prison and they're offering me two and a half.

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:08.080
And I said, I'm going for it. I'm innocent. I'm going to prove I'm innocent. My lead attorney

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:17.600
who has a reputation for being very difficult to get along with, pulled me aside very angrily

00:31:18.720 --> 00:31:25.280
and said, you know what your problem is? Your problem is you think this is about justice

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:33.520
and it's not about justice. It's about mitigating damage, take the deal. And I realized at that moment

00:31:34.080 --> 00:31:41.040
that he's right. It's not about justice. It's not about who's right. It's not about public service.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:48.000
It's about accepting the fact that you're screwed and trying to come out of it

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:57.760
with the best possible ending. Now Julian hasn't been offered a deal. And so it's up to the rest of us

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.920
to keep up the pressure on the government. I'm going to say it for the third time. He should

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:11.600
be celebrated, not prosecuted. And so it's up to us to keep yelling at the likes of the

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:18.400
Washington Post in the New York Times that they're next if this prosecution continues to move forward.

00:32:18.400 --> 00:32:27.120
You know, another thing that has surprised me is I believed that at the very beginning of the

00:32:27.120 --> 00:32:36.640
Biden administration that there was an opportunity for the White House or for Merrick Garland to say,

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:42.560
you know what? This guy's been effectively imprisoned for the last 12 years. We're just

00:32:42.560 --> 00:32:48.160
going to declare victory into home. Just walk away from the case. They proved their point.

00:32:49.360 --> 00:32:55.360
He's suicidal. They've broken him physically. Just declare victory and walk away.

00:32:56.560 --> 00:33:04.000
But they didn't walk away. They continued to prosecute him or to push for his prosecution.

00:33:04.960 --> 00:33:11.120
Now into the third presidential administration. I don't understand what the Biden administration

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:16.560
thinks they're going to accomplish from this. I just don't get it. If they think that this is

00:33:16.560 --> 00:33:23.200
going to force people to reconsider the idea of becoming a whistleblower or force people to

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:31.280
reconsider sending documents to WikiLeaks, on the contrary, this is what motivates us

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:38.480
to cooperate with WikiLeaks, to support Julian Assange, to blow the whistle when we see wrongdoing

00:33:38.480 --> 00:33:43.600
or waste, fraud, abuse and illegality threats to the public health and public safety,

00:33:43.600 --> 00:33:48.160
which is the legal definition of whistleblowing. If they think this is going to frighten people

00:33:48.160 --> 00:33:54.160
away, they're entirely wrong. It's going to motivate us. I definitely agree. I mean,

00:33:54.160 --> 00:34:00.880
I think even to see this is a different example in a different way, but even to see the whistleblowers

00:34:00.960 --> 00:34:06.000
that blew the whistle at Wells Fargo, which is kind of a very separate thing, but the Wells

00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:10.720
Fargo about the kind of predatory loan practices that they were doing. I mean, if it wasn't for

00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:17.200
them, who knows. I mean, after 2009 and the housing market collapsed and the type of practices

00:34:17.200 --> 00:34:22.480
that were going on, who knows where as everyday people we could be. I mean, the work of what

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:29.280
a whistleblower does, the exposing of the truth is so profoundly important to this country

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:33.680
on every way, shape and form. And so I think it's really profound that you say it's not about

00:34:33.680 --> 00:34:38.560
justice because I think it's important to name that. The system is not a just system. There's no

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:43.760
element, frankly, at all, in any way that we could say the United States is a just system.

00:34:43.760 --> 00:34:49.040
Just look at the Supreme Court. Look at what's going on right now. Even with the fundamental

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:54.240
idea of the fact that Donald Trump was president. He did not have a mandate from the people to

00:34:54.240 --> 00:34:58.720
be president. How can we call that justice in the system that's not built for justice? And

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:02.960
as you're talking, I'm thinking also about the case of Leonard Peltier, a political prisoner.

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:10.880
He's an old man. He's an old man who, for whatever it's worth, I mean, absolutely

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:16.240
is innocent on all levels, but he's an old man. What are they doing keeping him behind bars?

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:22.400
And it just goes to show how sick the system is because they want, it isn't even just about

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:26.000
punishment. On some level, I have a feeling it's personal. The same way they go after

00:35:26.000 --> 00:35:30.800
Assata Shakur. I mean, the state troopers in New Jersey have a very personal vendetta.

00:35:30.800 --> 00:35:35.520
I think there's just something so much deeper. I don't know, like so many things. Yeah.

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:41.520
Oh, Mumia too and Philly. I mean, you look at how the cops there respond. Sure. Sure.

00:35:42.400 --> 00:35:49.600
I don't mean to sound cynical, but you're exactly right. It is personal because they

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:56.480
don't get promoted by not prosecuting you. They don't get promoted by not asking for the longest

